lazysite for Debian
===================

The one load-bearing principle: no root writes into site trees
---------------------------------------------------------------

Installing or upgrading this package only refreshes the host payload at
/usr/share/lazysite. It NEVER touches a site tree. Sites are created and
upgraded FROM that payload by the `lazysite` CLI, running AS THE SITE
USER, so every file in the site tree carries the right ownership from
the start - no chown-after repair pass:

    sudo -u siteuser lazysite provision \
        --docroot /home/siteuser/web/example.com/public_html \
        --cgibin  /home/siteuser/web/example.com/cgi-bin \
        --domain  example.com

`lazysite provision` and `lazysite upgrade` refuse to run as root by
design. The single exception is the fleet verb:

    sudo lazysite upgrade --all

which iterates the site registry (/etc/lazysite/sites.d/, one small
key=value file per site, written at provision time) and drops to each
site's owner via sudo per site.

The registry directory ships root-owned. If unprivileged provision runs
should self-register their sites, give the directory a writable group of
your choosing (e.g. a `lazysite-registry` group containing the site
accounts); otherwise `provision` prints the registry entry for the admin
to drop in place and continues.

Runtime: FastCGI pool (production) or plain CGI (fallback)
----------------------------------------------------------

Plain CGI keeps working exactly as before and needs nothing from this
section. The packaged production shape is one persistent FastCGI pool
per site (~150x faster on cache hits): install libfcgi-perl and
libfcgi-procmanager-perl (Recommends), then per site:

1. Create /etc/lazysite/pools/example.com.conf:

       DOCROOT=/home/siteuser/web/example.com/public_html
       USER=siteuser
       GROUP=www-data
       WORKERS=2
       MAX_REQUESTS=500

2. systemctl enable --now lazysite@example.com

   systemd cannot template User= from an instance name, so the unit
   starts /usr/share/lazysite/tools/lazysite-pool.pl as root; it binds
   /run/lazysite/example.com.sock, chowns it USER:GROUP 0660, drops
   privileges to USER, and execs the processor with the listen socket
   on fd 0 (the spawn-fcgi convention). No root remains in the request
   path. The pattern is documented in the unit file itself.

3. Point the web server at the socket, e.g. Apache mod_proxy_fcgi:

       SetHandler "proxy:unix:/run/lazysite/example.com.sock|fcgi://localhost/"

   or nginx: fastcgi_pass unix:/run/lazysite/example.com.sock;

On HestiaCP hosts, install the lazysite-hestia package instead of wiring
any of this by hand: it ships Apache web domain templates for both
patterns (lazysite-cgi / lazysite-fcgi) and the lazysite-hestia-domain
command that provisions a domain, registers it and enables its pool in
one step - see that package's README.Debian. Plain lazysite-apache /
lazysite-nginx glue packages remain future work.

